%N A400956 a(1)=1, a(2)=2. Thereafter a(n) is the smallest k such that gcd(k,a(n-1)) > 1 and the number of terms among a(1),...,a(n-2) that share a common factor with k is at most k-1.
%C A400956 Each prime p > 2 appears in p consecutive terms. In the terms studied on all but six occasions the prime p is introduced by a term 2*p. In the six exceptional cases it is introduced by 3*p, these primes being a(18) = 5, a(70) = 13, a(291) = 29, a(1213) = 73, a(1539) = 89, a(11044) = 269. It is unknown if more such primes appear.
%C A400956 The primes make up the lower of the lines in the graph; see the attached image. It initially appears that this line is not broken by any composites, but remarkably a(2543611) = 5969 = 47*127 which is lower than the previous and next prime, these being 6247 and 6257 respectively. This is the first time that a composite is less than the surrounding two primes. It is likely more such composites eventually appear.
%C A400956 The missing numbers begin 10, 12, 18, 26, 28, 30, 35, 36, 40, 42, 44, 48, 50, 54, ... .
%H A400956 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A400956/b400956.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400956 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A400956/a400956.png">Image of the first 5000000 terms</a>. The colors are graduated across the spectrum from red to violet to show the total number of non-distinct prime factors, with red being 1.
%e A400956 a(17) = 15 as as 15 shares a factor with a(16) = 9 and with nine other terms, and 1 <= 10 <= 15.
